      "updated": 1768047730886,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How do we improve the booking experience by reserving tickets?\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Pessimistic Locking\n\n→ Lock specific ticket row, ensuring exclusive access to the first user trying to book it.\n→ Done using the SELECT FOR UPDATE statement\n→ Row lock is maintained until transaction is either committed or rolled back\n\nChallenges\n→ database locks are meant to be used for short periods of time\n→ Holding it for 5 minutes, increases the risk of lock contention and deadlocks\n→ PostgreSQL doesn't support lock timeouts within transactions\n→ Implementing a timeout this way requires application logic\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Implicit Status with Status and Expiration Time\n\n→ Add addtional column- expiry_time in Tickets table\n→ Change status to AVAILABLE -> RESERVED & set expiration to +10 minutes\n→ When checking for AVAILABLE,\n    → Check for RESERVED EXPIRED as well\n\nChallenges\n→ Read operations going to be be slightly slower needing to filter on two values\n→ Tickets table is less legible for other consumers of data, (as some reservations are\nactually expired)\n→ Risk can be minimized by having a cron sweep to clean up RESERVED EXPIRED\nentries\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Distributed Lock with TTL\n\n→ User acquires lock in Redis using ticket ID, with TTL. (This acts as automatic\nexpiration time)\n→ If purchase completes, ticket's status is updated to \"Booked\", & Redis lock is\nmanually released by application code\n→ If TTL expires (user did not complete purchase in time), Redis automatically releases\nlock\n→ Ticket table has two states: available | booked\n\nChallenges\n→ If Redis is down, during high contention, First to complete the payment will get the\ntickets reserved for them using OCC.\n→ During redis downtime, some users will face failed transactions and need to be\nrefunded, but there will be no double booking",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How do we improve the booking experience by reserving tickets?\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Pessimistic Locking\n\n→ Lock specific ticket row, ensuring exclusive access to the first user trying to book it.\n→ Done using the SELECT FOR UPDATE statement\n→ Row lock is maintained until transaction is either committed or rolled back\n\nChallenges\n→ database locks are meant to be used for short periods of time\n→ Holding it for 5 minutes, increases the risk of lock contention and deadlocks\n→ PostgreSQL doesn't support lock timeouts within transactions\n→ Implementing a timeout this way requires application logic\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Implicit Status with Status and Expiration Time\n\n→ Add addtional column- expiry_time in Tickets table\n→ Change status to AVAILABLE -> RESERVED & set expiration to +10 minutes\n→ When checking for AVAILABLE,\n    → Check for RESERVED EXPIRED as well\n\nChallenges\n→ Read operations going to be be slightly slower needing to filter on two values\n→ Tickets table is less legible for other consumers of data, (as some reservations are actually expired)\n→ Risk can be minimized by having a cron sweep to clean up RESERVED EXPIRED entries\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Distributed Lock with TTL\n\n→ User acquires lock in Redis using ticket ID, with TTL. (This acts as automatic expiration time)\n→ If purchase completes, ticket's status is updated to \"Booked\", & Redis lock is manually released by application code\n→ If TTL expires (user did not complete purchase in time), Redis automatically releases lock\n→ Ticket table has two states: available | booked\n\nChallenges\n→ If Redis is down, during high contention, First to complete the payment will get the tickets reserved for them using OCC.\n→ During redis downtime, some users will face failed transactions and need to be refunded, but there will be no double booking",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
