    {
      "id": "B7Uwew7XJ8tmoNj9YWxe1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4590.789873875254,
      "y": 3895.258913362457,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 1125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1259028758,
      "version": 3542,
      "versionNonce": 214999381,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1768056265631,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "4) How can you improve search to ensure we meet our low latency requirements?\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Indexing & SQL Query Optimization\n\n→ Create indexes on Event, Performer, and Venues tables\n→ Columns that are frequently used in queries (event name, event date, performer\nname, and venue location)\n→ Optimize queries to improve performance:\n    → Using EXPLAIN to analyze & optimize query execution plans\n    → Avoiding SELECT * queries (all columns)\n    → Using LIMIT to restrict number of rows returned\n    → UNION instead of OR to combine multiple queries\n\nChallenges\n→ We can only have optimized, full/exact string matches\n→ Standard indexes are less effective for partial string/word matches\n    → Arranges strings alphabetically, word/substring is unable to use the index\n    → Results in full table scan and full string searches\n→ There are options that might be more expensive, (need more space & compution to\nmaintain), eg: we could create inverted indexes on the text columns instead\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Full-text Indexes in the DB\n\n→ Use full-text indexes extensions which internally utilize search engines like Lucene\n\nChallenges\n→ Full text indexes require additional storage space also are slower to query than\nstandard indexes\n→ More difficult to maintain, as they require special handling in both queries and in\nmaintaining the database\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Use a Full-text Search Engine like Elasticsearch\n\n→ Elasticsearch excels in full-text (keyword) search, complex query execution, &\nhandling high-volume traffic efficiently\n→ Supports fuzzy search functionality, which allows for error tolerance in search queries\n(\"Taylor Swift\" vs \"Tayler Swift\").\n\nChallenges\n→ Keeping the Elasticsearch index synchronized with PostgreSQL is complex and\nrequires a reliable mechanism to ensure data consistency\n→ Maintaining Elasticsearch cluster adds additional infrastructure complexity and cost",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4) How can you improve search to ensure we meet our low latency requirements?\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Indexing & SQL Query Optimization\n\n→ Create indexes on Event, Performer, and Venues tables\n→ Columns that are frequently used in queries (event name, event date, performer name, and venue location)\n→ Optimize queries to improve performance:\n    → Using EXPLAIN to analyze & optimize query execution plans\n    → Avoiding SELECT * queries (all columns)\n    → Using LIMIT to restrict number of rows returned\n    → UNION instead of OR to combine multiple queries\n\nChallenges\n→ We can only have optimized, full/exact string matches\n→ Standard indexes are less effective for partial string/word matches\n    → Arranges strings alphabetically, word/substring is unable to use the index\n    → Results in full table scan and full string searches\n→ There are options that might be more expensive, (need more space & compution to maintain), eg: we could create inverted indexes on the text columns instead\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Full-text Indexes in the DB\n\n→ Use full-text indexes extensions which internally utilize search engines like Lucene\n\nChallenges\n→ Full text indexes require additional storage space also are slower to query than standard indexes\n→ More difficult to maintain, as they require special handling in both queries and in maintaining the database\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Use a Full-text Search Engine like Elasticsearch\n\n→ Elasticsearch excels in full-text (keyword) search, complex query execution, & handling high-volume traffic efficiently\n→ Supports fuzzy search functionality, which allows for error tolerance in search queries (\"Taylor Swift\" vs \"Tayler Swift\").\n\nChallenges\n→ Keeping the Elasticsearch index synchronized with PostgreSQL is complex and requires a reliable mechanism to ensure data consistency\n→ Maintaining Elasticsearch cluster adds additional infrastructure complexity and cost",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1CRTzBa7L2VcWbC63eqIp",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 5479.757145973672,
      "y": 3747.6714383214803,
      "width": 1146.5461176613317,
      "height": 1585,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0H",
