        }
      ],
      "updated": 1768047505093,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "pT4-1Zq0KWBoUMSAg6Qrn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5484.757145973672,
      "y": 3815.1714383214803,
      "width": 1123.338623046875,
      "height": 1450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 740627862,
      "version": 627,
      "versionNonce": 1164562779,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1768047656209,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This advice is about query planning and index usage, and it’s very relevant in high-read systems like ticketing.\n\nShort version:\n\nUsing UNION (or UNION ALL) instead of OR often allows the database to use indexes efficiently instead of falling back to\nscans.\n\nWhy OR can be slow. Consider a typical availability query:\n\nSELECT *\nFROM tickets\nWHERE event_id = :eventId\n  AND (status = 'available' OR price < 50);\n\nEven with indexes, many RDBMSs struggle because:\n    → OR spans multiple predicates\n    → The optimizer may choose one index or none\n    → It may fall back to a bitmap or full scan\n\nThis is especially painful on hot, large tables.\n\nHow UNION helps, Rewrite the query as:\n\nSELECT *\nFROM tickets\nWHERE event_id = :eventId\n  AND status = 'available'\n\nUNION ALL\n\nSELECT *\nFROM tickets\nWHERE event_id = :eventId\n  AND price < 50;\n\nNow, Each subquery is simple and index-friendly. The optimizer can use (event_id, status) index for first. and (event_id, price)\nfor second. Results are merged afterward. Otherwise the planner can determine to do a full table scan.\n\nThis is dramatically more predictable under load.\n\nWhy this matters at scale\n    → Millions of concurrent reads\n    → Very skewed data (hot events)\n    → Tiny inefficiencies become outages\n\nThe goal is:\n    → Force the query planner into an index-only plan every time.\n\nWhen OR is okay\n    → Very small tables\n    → Low concurrency\n    → Columns covered by a single composite index and DB supports bitmap OR well (e.g., Postgres sometimes does)\n\nBut in mission-critical hot paths, UNION ALL is safer.\n\n→ Using UNION ALL instead of OR lets the database use indexes on each predicate independently, avoiding scans and\nmaking performance more predictable under high read load.\n→ OR is logically simple but physically expensive; UNION ALL is verbose but index-friendly and scalable.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "1CRTzBa7L2VcWbC63eqIp",
      "originalText": "This advice is about query planning and index usage, and it’s very relevant in high-read systems like ticketing.\n\nShort version:\n\nUsing UNION (or UNION ALL) instead of OR often allows the database to use indexes efficiently instead of falling back to scans.\n\nWhy OR can be slow. Consider a typical availability query:\n\nSELECT *\nFROM tickets\nWHERE event_id = :eventId\n  AND (status = 'available' OR price < 50);\n\nEven with indexes, many RDBMSs struggle because:\n    → OR spans multiple predicates\n    → The optimizer may choose one index or none\n    → It may fall back to a bitmap or full scan\n\nThis is especially painful on hot, large tables.\n\nHow UNION helps, Rewrite the query as:\n\nSELECT *\nFROM tickets\nWHERE event_id = :eventId\n  AND status = 'available'\n\nUNION ALL\n\nSELECT *\nFROM tickets\nWHERE event_id = :eventId\n  AND price < 50;\n\nNow, Each subquery is simple and index-friendly. The optimizer can use (event_id, status) index for first. and (event_id, price) for second. Results are merged afterward. Otherwise the planner can determine to do a full table scan.\n\nThis is dramatically more predictable under load.\n\nWhy this matters at scale\n    → Millions of concurrent reads\n    → Very skewed data (hot events)\n    → Tiny inefficiencies become outages\n\nThe goal is:\n    → Force the query planner into an index-only plan every time.\n\nWhen OR is okay\n    → Very small tables\n    → Low concurrency\n    → Columns covered by a single composite index and DB supports bitmap OR well (e.g., Postgres sometimes does)\n\nBut in mission-critical hot paths, UNION ALL is safer.\n\n→ Using UNION ALL instead of OR lets the database use indexes on each predicate independently, avoiding scans and making performance more predictable under high read load.\n→ OR is logically simple but physically expensive; UNION ALL is verbose but index-friendly and scalable.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
