      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "5) How can you speed up frequently repeated search queries and reduce load on our\nsearch infrastructure?\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Implement Caching Strategies Using Redis or Memcached\n\n→ Use Redis or Memcached to store results of frequent search queries\n    → Construct cache keys based on search query parameters\n    → Set appropriate TTLs to ensure freshness and relevance of info\n\n{\n  \"key\": \"search:keyword=Taylor Swift&start=2021-01-01&end=2021-12-31\",\n  \"value\": [event1, event2, event3],\n  \"ttl\": 60 * 60 * 24 // 24 hours\n}\n\nChallenges\n→ Managing cache invalidation is challenging, stale/outdated data leads to incorrect\nsearch results\n→ But can be achieved by using a combination of TTLs and cache invalidation triggers \n    → TTL: Cache self-heals even if invalidation fails, but only guarantees eventual\ncorrectness.\n    → Invalidation triggers based on cache tags, can ensure data consistency\n→ Fuzzy search results add additional complexity when caching\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Implement Query Result Caching and Edge Caching Techniques\n\n→ Elasticsearch has built-in caching capabilities, that account for creating and\nmaintaining consistency of cache entries as well as keeping hot entries through LRU\n→ It also maintains cache entries for fuzzy searches\n→ The primary advantage is that we don't have to take any responsibility for cache\nmanagement.\n→ CDNs can be used to cache search results geographically, reducing latency and\nimproving response times.\n→ Only for results, that are not personalized (i.e) same result for every user\n\nChallenges\n→ Full text indexes require additional storage space also are slower to query than\nstandard indexes\n→ Requires more infrastructure, including Elasticsearch cluster, CDC & integration with\nCDNs",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "5) How can you speed up frequently repeated search queries and reduce load on our search infrastructure?\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Implement Caching Strategies Using Redis or Memcached\n\n→ Use Redis or Memcached to store results of frequent search queries\n    → Construct cache keys based on search query parameters\n    → Set appropriate TTLs to ensure freshness and relevance of info\n\n{\n  \"key\": \"search:keyword=Taylor Swift&start=2021-01-01&end=2021-12-31\",\n  \"value\": [event1, event2, event3],\n  \"ttl\": 60 * 60 * 24 // 24 hours\n}\n\nChallenges\n→ Managing cache invalidation is challenging, stale/outdated data leads to incorrect search results\n→ But can be achieved by using a combination of TTLs and cache invalidation triggers \n    → TTL: Cache self-heals even if invalidation fails, but only guarantees eventual correctness.\n    → Invalidation triggers based on cache tags, can ensure data consistency\n→ Fuzzy search results add additional complexity when caching\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Implement Query Result Caching and Edge Caching Techniques\n\n→ Elasticsearch has built-in caching capabilities, that account for creating and maintaining consistency of cache entries as well as keeping hot entries through LRU\n→ It also maintains cache entries for fuzzy searches\n→ The primary advantage is that we don't have to take any responsibility for cache management.\n→ CDNs can be used to cache search results geographically, reducing latency and improving response times.\n→ Only for results, that are not personalized (i.e) same result for every user\n\nChallenges\n→ Full text indexes require additional storage space also are slower to query than standard indexes\n→ Requires more infrastructure, including Elasticsearch cluster, CDC & integration with CDNs",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "6ge5mCV8_J_ef9tl4cTfL",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 2782.535048616879,
      "y": 5231.162915458952,
      "width": 901.5425345661774,
      "height": 2599.5303405418063,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0K",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 568412938,
      "version": 697,
      "versionNonce": 1596603541,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "PsgRIDCy1GyqdAwJesS53"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1768047509639,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "PsgRIDCy1GyqdAwJesS53",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2787.535048616879,
      "y": 5293.4280857298545,
      "width": 867.25927734375,
      "height": 2475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2052544970,
      "version": 1126,
      "versionNonce": 467350235,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1768056850674,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The core idea is-\n\nDon’t rely on just one mechanism to keep cache data fresh. Use:\n    → TTLs to guarantee eventual cleanup\n    → Invalidation triggers to remove stale data quickly\n    → Cache tags to invalidate groups of related cache entries efficiently\n\nTogether, they balance performance, correctness, and operability.\n\n\n1. TTLs (Time-to-Live) ⏱️\n\n→ Each cached item has an expiry time:\n\nseatmap:event:E123 → TTL = 30s\n\n→ TTLs guarantee\n    → Stale data cannot live forever\n    → Cache self-heals even if invalidation fails\n→ TTLs do NOT guarantee\n    → Immediate consistency\n    → Freshness after a write\n\n→ TTL alone = eventual correctness, not real-time accuracy.\n\n\n2. Cache invalidation triggers 🔔\n\n→ When something important changes:\n    → Seat booked\n    → Price updated\n    → Event cancelled\n→ Write path emits an invalidation event:\n\nEVENT: seat_booked\npayload: { eventId: E123, seatId: A-10-3 }\n\n→ This tells caches: “Some cached data is now wrong.”\n\n\n3. Cache tags 🏷️ (the key enabler)\n\n→ Instead of tracking individual/entire cache keys, tags are added to cache entries:\n\ncache_key: seatmap:event:E123\ntags: [event:E123, venue:V9]\n\ncache_key: availability:event:E123:section:A\ntags: [event:E123, section:A]\n\n→ Entries can be invalidated by tag:\n\ninvalidate tag: event:E123\n\n→ all related cache entries are removed at once, avoiding “what exact key do I delete?” problem.\n\n\n4. Why you need all three together\n\nMechanism                      Solves                                               Doesn’t solve\n\nTTL                                   Zombie cache entries                      Immediate freshness\nInvalidation                      Fast correctness                              Missed events\nCache tags                       Scalable invalidation                      Orphaned data\n\n\nUsing all three:\n→ TTL is the safety net\n→ Invalidation is the fast path\n→ Tags make invalidation manageable\n\n\nTicketmaster example 🎟️\n\nRead path\n\nGET /events/E123/seats\n→ CDN / Redis cache\n\nWrite path\n\nPOST /bookings\n→ seat A-10-3 sold\n→ emit \"seat_booked\" event\n→ invalidate tag event:E123\n\nIf invalidation succeeds → instant freshness\nIf it fails → TTL expires shortly after\nNo seat stays stale forever.\n\n\nInterview-ready rephrasing 🎯\n→ We combine short TTLs with event-driven cache invalidation using cache tags, so writes can\npromptly evict stale data while TTLs act as a fallback to prevent permanent inconsistency.\n\n\nOne-line takeaway\n→ TTL guarantees eventual correctness, invalidation provides fast correctness, and cache tags\nmake invalidation scalable.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "6ge5mCV8_J_ef9tl4cTfL",
      "originalText": "The core idea is-\n\nDon’t rely on just one mechanism to keep cache data fresh. Use:\n    → TTLs to guarantee eventual cleanup\n    → Invalidation triggers to remove stale data quickly\n    → Cache tags to invalidate groups of related cache entries efficiently\n\nTogether, they balance performance, correctness, and operability.\n\n\n1. TTLs (Time-to-Live) ⏱️\n\n→ Each cached item has an expiry time:\n\nseatmap:event:E123 → TTL = 30s\n\n→ TTLs guarantee\n    → Stale data cannot live forever\n    → Cache self-heals even if invalidation fails\n→ TTLs do NOT guarantee\n    → Immediate consistency\n    → Freshness after a write\n\n→ TTL alone = eventual correctness, not real-time accuracy.\n\n\n2. Cache invalidation triggers 🔔\n\n→ When something important changes:\n    → Seat booked\n    → Price updated\n    → Event cancelled\n→ Write path emits an invalidation event:\n\nEVENT: seat_booked\npayload: { eventId: E123, seatId: A-10-3 }\n\n→ This tells caches: “Some cached data is now wrong.”\n\n\n3. Cache tags 🏷️ (the key enabler)\n\n→ Instead of tracking individual/entire cache keys, tags are added to cache entries:\n\ncache_key: seatmap:event:E123\ntags: [event:E123, venue:V9]\n\ncache_key: availability:event:E123:section:A\ntags: [event:E123, section:A]\n\n→ Entries can be invalidated by tag:\n\ninvalidate tag: event:E123\n\n→ all related cache entries are removed at once, avoiding “what exact key do I delete?” problem.\n\n\n4. Why you need all three together\n\nMechanism                      Solves                                               Doesn’t solve\n\nTTL                                   Zombie cache entries                      Immediate freshness\nInvalidation                      Fast correctness                              Missed events\nCache tags                       Scalable invalidation                      Orphaned data\n\n\nUsing all three:\n→ TTL is the safety net\n→ Invalidation is the fast path\n→ Tags make invalidation manageable\n\n\nTicketmaster example 🎟️\n\nRead path\n\nGET /events/E123/seats\n→ CDN / Redis cache\n\nWrite path\n\nPOST /bookings\n→ seat A-10-3 sold\n→ emit \"seat_booked\" event\n→ invalidate tag event:E123\n\nIf invalidation succeeds → instant freshness\nIf it fails → TTL expires shortly after\nNo seat stays stale forever.\n\n\nInterview-ready rephrasing 🎯\n→ We combine short TTLs with event-driven cache invalidation using cache tags, so writes can promptly evict stale data while TTLs act as a fallback to prevent permanent inconsistency.\n\n\nOne-line takeaway\n→ TTL guarantees eventual correctness, invalidation provides fast correctness, and cache tags make invalidation scalable.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
